The path from src/llm/llm.c:897 to the module's done delivery is not covered by any suite: t/app/test_assistant.c never drives an OP delivery. The producer stamp that canlin#88 added is therefore covered by no test - removing the stamp would break the assistant's tool results and nothing would fail. The card asks for a test that drives an llm result through the delivery into the module and asserts it arrives as a done event with its value. Found by the 2026-11-14 work on k88.

2026-11-14 21:50 Test written, both ends of the delivery observed.

Where and why: t/llm/test_llm.c gets the dedicated test (the LLM lane's suite is where the producer and the module seam meet; it already has the real sandbox, the real module, the fixture transport and the fake clock). t/module/test_module.c was the alternative and is wrong for this: mk/module.mk says the module lane needs ev, lua, render, sandbox and ui - the llm lane needs the module lane (mk/llm.mk), so a test that drives the llm producer cannot live in the module suite without inverting that dependency.

- t/llm/test_llm.c: new test_result_reaches_the_module(). A module in the real sandbox watches for its own done event and prints the whole payload: the handle of its request, the ok flag and every field of the value. Three runs against the fixture transport: a plain answer (value {stop='end'}), a cut-off answer (value {stop='length', dropped_tools=1} - both fields of the table) and a failed answer (ok=false with the producer's code and message). It asserts the handle matches the request, that exactly one done event arrives, that the value's key set is exactly what the producer sent and the types of the fields.
- t/app/test_assistant.c: test_conversation now asserts that the real built-in assistant module got the done event with its value ('turn ok tool', 'turn ok end'). Before, only the negative case was checked there.

